  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "The El Niño–Southern Oscillation (ENSO) is a natural, irregular climate cycle involving alternating warm (El Niño) and cold (La Niña) ocean surface temperatures in the tropical Pacific, accompanied by shifts in atmospheric pressure. It typically impacts global weather patterns every two to seven years, driving extreme events like floods and droughts, often causing severe agricultural disruption. This video explains what the El Niño–Southern Oscillation is:55sDr GilbzYouTube • Apr 14, 2020 55s Key Aspects of ENSO Types/Phases: ENSO consists of three phases: El Niño (warm phase, weakened trade winds), La Niña (cold phase, strong trade winds), and Neutral. Functions/Mechanism: It is a coupled phenomenon, meaning changes in the Pacific Ocean temperatures affect the atmosphere (wind/pressure), which in turn changes ocean temperatures, creating a feedback loop. Global Impacts: El Niño often brings wetter conditions to the southern U.S. and drier weather to Australia and Southeast Asia, while La Niña typically causes the reverse. Benefits and Risks: While mostly known for hazards, cooler La Niña waters can increase fish populations, while warmer El Niño waters can reduce Atlantic hurricane activity. Controls/Predictions: While humanity cannot control the cycle, institutions like NOAA use the Southern Oscillation Index (SOI) to predict these events and provide early warnings to mitigate impacts, particularly for agriculture.",
